  "query_preview": "..Drier air continues to filter into the region under\nfairly weak southwesterly flow, resulting in quieter conditions\nthis weekend across Utah and southwest Wyoming. Still, rainfall \nover the past few days has resulted in a relatively moist boundary\nlayer, with dewpoints this morning still in the 50s across many \nareas. With this low-level moisture, isolated showers and \nthunderstorms will redevelop this afternoon, primarily over higher\nterrain and over far southwestern Utah. The flash flood threat is\nmuch lower today as the air mass trends drier, but a Flash Flood\nPotential Rating of Possible is still in place today for Zion and\nSnow Canyon given slow storm motions and this lingering low-level\nmoisture.\n\nWhile a ridge across the Southern Plains will still be the main\nlarge-scale feature in our forecast, a shortwave trough moving\nthrough the Interior Northwest on Tuesday into Wednesday could act\nto enhance southwesterly flow and draw moisture/thunderstorm\nchances across northern Utah, particularly on Tuesday afternoon.\nForecast uncertainty increases thereafter, though model guidance\ndoes favor high pressure strengthening once again over the Four\nCorners region, which could bring back monsoonal moisture as early\nas Wednesday (but likely Thursday or later). With strengthening\nhigh pressure, temperatures will also slowly increase back to\naround normal by mid-week.",
